1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,873
<font color="#000000">Conditional Matrix Flows for Gaussian Graphical
Models, from the University of Basel,</font>

2
00:00:05,953 --> 00:00:07,990
<font color="#000000">unifies sparse network inference.</font>

3
00:00:08,070 --> 00:00:13,764
<font color="#000000">With one trained matrix normalizing flow it
delivers the full Bayesian posterior for every</font>

4
00:00:13,844 --> 00:00:16,972
<font color="#000000">regularization strength and every l-q pseudo-norm,</font>

5
00:00:17,052 --> 00:00:19,794
<font color="#000000">the marginal likelihood for model selection,</font>

6
00:00:19,874 --> 00:00:23,772
<font color="#000000">and the frequentist solution path through simulated
annealing.</font>

7
00:00:24,152 --> 00:00:29,482
<font color="#000000">Gaussian Graphical Models read conditional
independence from the precision matrix,</font>

8
00:00:29,562 --> 00:00:35,222
<font color="#000000">the inverse covariance: a zero entry means two
variables are conditionally independent,</font>

9
00:00:35,302 --> 00:00:38,520
<font color="#000000">so a sparse precision matrix recovers the network.</font>

10
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:43,534
<font color="#000000">The ideal l-zero penalty that counts edges is
combinatorial and intractable.</font>

11
00:00:43,614 --> 00:00:47,625
<font color="#000000">Sub-l-one pseudo-norms approximate it well but are
non-convex,</font>

12
00:00:47,705 --> 00:00:52,177
<font color="#000000">so most methods retreat to the convex l-one norm,
which over-shrinks,</font>

13
00:00:52,257 --> 00:00:55,080
<font color="#000000">especially when variables outnumber samples.</font>

14
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:59,105
<font color="#000000">Two camps exist.</font>

15
00:00:59,185 --> 00:01:02,831
<font color="#000000">The frequentist Graphical Lasso traces the whole
solution path as lambda varies,</font>

16
00:01:02,911 --> 00:01:06,556
<font color="#000000">but returns only point estimates.</font>

17
00:01:06,636 --> 00:01:10,281
<font color="#000000">The Bayesian Graphical Lasso gives a full posterior
and a marginal likelihood,</font>

18
00:01:10,361 --> 00:01:14,007
<font color="#000000">but relies on Gibbs samplers that mix poorly and
must restart for every lambda.</font>

19
00:01:14,087 --> 00:01:17,732
<font color="#000000">Variational inference could bridge them,</font>

20
00:01:17,812 --> 00:01:21,457
<font color="#000000">yet the usual mean-field trick assumes independence,</font>

21
00:01:21,537 --> 00:01:25,183
<font color="#000000">exactly what a graphical model seeks.</font>

22
00:01:25,263 --> 00:01:28,908
<font color="#000000">We need a family that respects dependence and
flexes across lambda and the norm.</font>

23
00:01:29,288 --> 00:01:32,361
<font color="#000000">The flow works on the Cholesky factorization.</font>

24
00:01:32,441 --> 00:01:36,005
<font color="#000000">Any positive-definite matrix is L times L-transpose,</font>

25
00:01:36,085 --> 00:01:41,961
<font color="#000000">so a d-dimensional precision matrix needs only d
times d plus one, over two, numbers.</font>

26
00:01:42,041 --> 00:01:46,235
<font color="#000000">The flow pushes a base vector through
Sum-of-Sigmoids layers,</font>

27
00:01:46,315 --> 00:01:52,051
<font color="#000000">reshapes it into a triangular matrix, forces the
diagonal positive with a softplus,</font>

28
00:01:52,131 --> 00:01:54,223
<font color="#000000">and takes the Cholesky product,</font>

29
00:01:54,303 --> 00:01:58,777
<font color="#000000">so every output is a valid precision matrix with
cheap Jacobians.</font>

30
00:01:58,857 --> 00:02:01,370
<font color="#000000">A hypernetwork feeds in lambda and q,</font>

31
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:08,657
<font color="#000000">and training minimizes the KL divergence to the
unnormalized posterior under a generalized-Normal prior.</font>

32
00:02:08,737 --> 00:02:12,371
<font color="#000000">Lasso appears at q equals one, Ridge at q equals two,</font>

33
00:02:12,451 --> 00:02:15,384
<font color="#000000">and smaller q reaches the sub-l-one regime.</font>

34
00:02:15,764 --> 00:02:19,212
<font color="#000000">Experiments use two data types.</font>

35
00:02:19,292 --> 00:02:22,740
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

36
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:26,268
<font color="#000000">synthetic sparse precision matrices from
Scikit-learn: a small fifteen-by-fifteen case to visualize posteriors,</font>

37
00:02:26,348 --> 00:02:29,796
<font color="#000000">and a thirty-dimensional setting with fifteen to
forty-five samples for edge recovery,</font>

38
00:02:29,876 --> 00:02:33,324
<font color="#000000">averaged over ten ground-truth matrices.</font>

39
00:02:33,404 --> 00:02:36,852
<font color="#000000">Second,</font>

40
00:02:36,932 --> 00:02:40,380
<font color="#000000">a real colorectal-cancer dataset with six clinical
variables and three hundred twelve</font>

41
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:43,908
<font color="#000000">gene-expression measurements across one hundred
ninety patients,</font>

42
00:02:43,988 --> 00:02:47,436
<font color="#000000">inferring only the relevant sub-blocks of the
precision matrix.</font>

43
00:02:47,816 --> 00:02:51,197
<font color="#000000">One model does the work of many.</font>

44
00:02:51,277 --> 00:02:54,658
<font color="#000000">Trained once,</font>

45
00:02:54,738 --> 00:02:58,118
<font color="#000000">the flow reconstructs the frequentist solution path
with a mean squared error of just zero point zero five two.</font>

46
00:02:58,198 --> 00:03:01,579
<font color="#000000">When samples are scarce,</font>

47
00:03:01,659 --> 00:03:05,040
<font color="#000000">the sub-l-one pseudo-norms clearly beat the
Bayesian Graphical Lasso,</font>

48
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:08,501
<font color="#000000">and the gain grows as q approaches zero.</font>

49
00:03:08,581 --> 00:03:11,962
<font color="#000000">Against frequentist competitors it wins at every
sample size,</font>

50
00:03:12,042 --> 00:03:15,422
<font color="#000000">most at fifteen samples.</font>

51
00:03:15,502 --> 00:03:18,883
<font color="#000000">Its model selection picks a lambda of three point
five two,</font>

52
00:03:18,963 --> 00:03:22,344
<font color="#000000">matching the cross-validated Graphical Lasso value
of three point three six.</font>

53
00:03:22,724 --> 00:03:25,802
<font color="#000000">The key study varies the pseudo-norm exponent q.</font>

54
00:03:25,882 --> 00:03:28,367
<font color="#000000">As q shrinks from one toward a quarter,</font>

55
00:03:28,447 --> 00:03:32,973
<font color="#000000">the posterior median for a precision entry is
pulled less toward zero,</font>

56
00:03:33,053 --> 00:03:35,341
<font color="#000000">reducing over-shrinkage as promised.</font>

57
00:03:35,421 --> 00:03:42,183
<font color="#000000">At a lambda of zero point three the ninety-five
percent credible interval excludes zero for q below one,</font>

58
00:03:42,263 --> 00:03:47,775
<font color="#000000">while the Bayesian Lasso at q equals one still
includes zero and would drop the edge.</font>

59
00:03:47,855 --> 00:03:49,814
<font color="#000000">A second knob, the temperature,</font>

60
00:03:49,894 --> 00:03:54,156
<font color="#000000">slides the model from the Bayesian regime to the
frequentist path.</font>

61
00:03:54,536 --> 00:03:58,178
<font color="#000000">The takeaway: one normalizing flow can replace a toolbox.</font>

62
00:03:58,258 --> 00:04:05,035
<font color="#000000">Built on positive-definite matrices and conditioned
on both the shrinkage strength and the norm exponent,</font>

63
00:04:05,115 --> 00:04:09,541
<font color="#000000">it delivers Bayesian posteriors,
marginal-likelihood model selection,</font>

64
00:04:09,621 --> 00:04:14,242
<font color="#000000">and the frequentist solution path, for every
setting and every l-q norm,</font>

65
00:04:14,322 --> 00:04:17,834
<font color="#000000">including the non-convex sub-l-one region others avoid.</font>

66
00:04:17,914 --> 00:04:20,642
<font color="#000000">Edge recovery improves where it is hardest,</font>

67
00:04:20,722 --> 00:04:24,168
<font color="#000000">and sampling runs hundreds of times faster than Gibbs.</font>
